            <ArticleTitle>Angina pectoris from an unexpected cause.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>441-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 53 year-old-man developed classical angina pectoris, presumably the result of coronary atherosclerosis. Thirty-two years before, an abnormality on a chest film had been described as a &quot;prominence of the upper portion of the left cardiac border.&quot; The &quot;prominence&quot; was actually an angiosarcoma, which eventually compromised blood flow in the left coronary system.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
